WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.430
- The labor market is incredibly strong.

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:04.680
- [Narrator] It might seem like
Fed Chairman Jerome Powell's

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:06.360
view of the labor market doesn't jive

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:09.630
with the layoff after layoff after layoff

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:11.430
that is plagued to the tech industry.

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:13.830
However, he's right, at least according

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:16.950
to the Labor Department's
recent jobs reports.

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:21.450
Employers added over 260,000
new positions in November,

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:25.200
the 23rd month in a row
the economy has added them,

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:29.340
and unemployment is under 4%,
near the lowest on record.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.610
- So this is a great labor
market in that sense.

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:34.350
- There's this huge disconnect.

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:35.490
You're hearing about companies

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:39.450
like Amazon, Twitter,
DoorDash layoff workers,

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:41.790
and it feels like we're in
the middle of a recession.

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:43.650
- [Narrator] Here's
why those big headlines

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:46.144
aren't in sync with
the overall job market.

00:00:46.144 --> 00:00:47.640
(light somber music)

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:49.590
First, some of the recent layoffs

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:51.810
aren't yet reflected in the data.

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:55.830
For example, about 1.4
million people were laid off

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:58.290
or discharged from their jobs in November,

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:00.960
but that number may not
include the entire month.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.700
- Some of this data does come with a lag.

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:06.090
For example, some companies may
have announced these layoffs

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:09.180
too late in the month for them
to be captured in the data.

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:12.210
- [Narrator] For example,
DoorDash's cuts on November 30th

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:14.280
weren't reflected in the report,

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:19.280
neither were Amazon's on the
16th or Carvana's on the 18th.

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:22.800
Also, the Labor Department
counts the layoffs themselves,

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:24.510
not the announcements.

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:26.130
- Companies may be saying

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:28.710
that they're going to tighten
their belts and lay people off

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:31.320
but they simply haven't
made those cuts quite yet.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.240
So that can lead to that lag.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:35.340
- [Narrator] The delay
might be part of the reason

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:37.200
the unemployment rate has stayed...

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.070
- Near an all-time 50-year low: 3.7%

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:42.870
- [Narrator] But there is more to it.

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:43.800
- The jobs market

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:46.650
is in a really really
weird place right now,

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:48.690
and there's this historic gap

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:51.330
between the number of workers
looking for employment

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:54.000
and the number of jobs out there.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:54.833
- [Narrator] That gap

00:01:54.833 --> 00:01:57.570
is partly driven by a shrinking workforce.

00:01:57.570 --> 00:01:59.910
- The labor-force participation rate,

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:02.550
the number of people out
there looking for jobs

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:05.700
and wanting to have a
job, is historically low

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:08.280
and actually ticked down in November.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:10.410
- [Narrator] One reason why
fewer people are looking

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:12.390
is that millions left the workforce

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:15.990
during the Covid 19
pandemic starting in 2020.

00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:19.560
Roughly 2.6 million retired early

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:22.200
and more people retired in 2020

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.660
than had in the previous eight years.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:27.750
Others left for reasons
like lack of childcare.

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:30.360
Now there's about 1.7 job openings

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:32.760
for every person looking for work.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:35.400
- So what we're seeing
is intense competition

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.530
for workers right now
because you have this gap

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:39.240
between the number of jobs out there

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:41.040
and the number of people looking for jobs

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:44.130
and that helps add to this
low unemployment rate.

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:46.320
- [Narrator] While Covid
is affecting unemployment,

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:49.470
tech layoffs aren't big enough to register

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:52.680
but data quirks are just one factor.

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:55.920
Another is that the job cuts
are proportionally smaller

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.710
than the headlines might have you believe.

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:02.310
Looking at the numbers, Amazon
and Meta announced layoffs

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:06.210
of about 10,000 and 11,000 each.

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:09.600
Snap said they'd cut 1,200 jobs.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:11.700
Shopify cited 1,000.

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:14.730
And Netflix estimated 300.

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:17.670
In the entire sector,
about 50,000 job cuts

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:19.368
were announced in November.

00:03:19.368 --> 00:03:20.201
(mouse clicks)

00:03:20.201 --> 00:03:21.750
To put that into perspective, there are

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:25.590
more than 150 million
people working in the US.

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:30.450
The entire tech industry
is just 3% of the whole.

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:33.630
- You'd think it'd be
companies in the S&amp;P 500,

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:37.170
the mega cap companies
driving job growth in the US,

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:39.630
but in fact, it's the small businesses

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:41.820
driving more of the job growth.

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:43.470
Companies within the S&amp;P 500

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:47.160
make up just 19% of employment in the US.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.260
- [Narrator] To be sure,
tech layoffs did count

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:52.320
for a large proportion
of November's job cuts.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:56.163
And this year there were more
than any year since 2002.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:59.670
- So these tech companies
are market leaders.

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:02.460
They are some of the
biggest companies in the US,

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:05.970
if not the entire world,
and they also loom large

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:07.320
over the public consciousness.

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:09.570
I mean, think about
the phones that we use,

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:12.690
the computers that we
use, where we online shop.

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:15.600
You know, they are leaders
in so many different ways.

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:17.760
However, they just don't make up

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:20.640
as big of a part of the
labor market as we think.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:22.140
- [Narrator] Other sectors that lost jobs

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:25.470
include retail and
transportation and warehousing.

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:26.850
But it's still unclear

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:30.300
whether layoffs will
continue in those sectors.

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:32.940
Economists expect that
additional interest rate hikes

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:36.810
from the Fed may trigger
more widespread layoffs.

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:40.260
- So the tech industry is
facing one of its biggest tests

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:42.990
and one of its biggest
downturns of the past decade.

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:44.580
As the slowdown continues,

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:47.282
we very well could see
more tech layoffs ahead.

00:04:47.282 --> 00:04:48.115
(register beeps)

00:04:48.115 --> 00:04:49.260
- [Narrator] And as
inflation remains high,

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:51.090
additional layoffs will depend

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:54.911
on how the job market
reacts to Fed policy.

00:04:54.911 --> 00:04:57.994
(light somber music)

